Evan: Deploying a tiny Next middleware that calls the JS SDK to Vercel Edge fails during build with "Module not found: Can't resolve 'fs'" and also runtime complains about Headers mutation. Anyone seen this combo?
Ria: yep, last week. Which SDK version?
Evan: the starter pinned to redwood-js-sdk@0.8.4. Build log shows token-cache.js importing fs at top-level and headers-merge util doing `orig.forEach(...)`.
Kay: two separate problems: 1) top-level Node imports (fs/stream/undici) get pulled into the edge bundle; 2) Headers handling assumes Node Fetch shapes and mutates the input. Next Edge's Headers can be immutable or slim.
Evan: quick repro in middleware: ```ts
import { createClient } from 'redwood-js-sdk'
export async function middleware(req: Request) {
  const c = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY })
  await c.generate({ prompt: 'hi' })
  return new Response('ok')
}
```
Ria: the fix we landed in the patch: A) split entrypoints (./edge and ./node) and set conditional exports so bundlers prefer the edge ESM; B) move node-only logic and imports behind runtime guards and dynamic imports; C) defensively normalize Headers.
Kay: package.json snippet we used: ```json
"exports": {
  "./edge": "./dist/edge.mjs",
  "./node": "./dist/node.cjs",
  "./": {
    "node": "./dist/node.cjs",
    "default": "./dist/edge.mjs"
  }
}
```
This gives projects an explicit small edge entry and prevents static analyzers from bundling node libs.
Evan: what about TS types? Our Next projects hit errors if types reference NodeJS types.
Ria: split types into dist/types/index.d.ts (DOM-friendly) and dist/types/node.d.ts. We add a typesVersions mapping and also export subpath types: "./edge": { "types": "dist/types/index.d.ts" }, "./node": { "types": "dist/types/node.d.ts" }.
Kay: Headers normalizer we added: ```js
export function normalizeHeaders(h) {
  if (!h) return {}
  if (typeof h.entries === 'function') return Object.fromEntries(h.entries())
  if (typeof h[Symbol.iterator] === 'function') return Object.fromEntries(h)
  return {...h}
}
```
and merge util always returns a fresh Headers or plain object rather than mutating the original.
Evan: good. Any gotchas with bundlers still pulling node code? Next's ESM analysis is aggressive.
Ria: yes — avoid any top-level `import 'node-only-lib'`. Put node-only code in a separate file and only dynamic import it inside a `if (IS_NODE)` block: ```js
const IS_NODE = typeof process !== 'undefined' && !!process.versions?.node
async function getNodeAdapter() {
  if (!IS_NODE) return null
  return await import('./node-adapter.js')
}
```
That prevents static analysis from including node-adapter in edge builds.
Kay: we also replaced direct references to Node builtins in shared modules. No Buffer, no NodeJS.Timeout, etc. Node-only helper lives in node-adapter and returns node-typed things.
Evan: short-term workaround for customers: import the edge entry explicitly and clone headers before passing them: ```ts
import { createClient } from 'redwood-js-sdk/edge'
export async function middleware(req: Request) {
  const headers = Object.fromEntries(req.headers)
  const c = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY })
  await c.generate({ prompt: 'x', headers })
}
```
Ria: also update starter README to recommend creating client inside the handler (not at module top-level) for Edge, and point to the edge subpath. Top-level init can trigger fs/env reads in some code paths.
Kay: CI change: added a tiny Next middleware fixture and a `next build` job on PRs so we catch bundling issues early. BuildBot: CI job created: middleware-edge-build-matrix.
Evan: thanks — I'll test the canary once it's published. DocsBot: please add a short FAQ entry: "Build error: Can't resolve 'fs'" -> upgrade to >=0.8.7 or import from '/edge' and check for top-level node imports in deps.
DocsBot: noted, drafting docs PR with migration steps and example middleware snippet.
Evan: awesome. If anyone hits an edge runtime oddity, share the build log and the SDK import path so we can triage.
